QB.add(28,[{"id":28001,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which statement correctly distinguishes first and second messengers?","o":["First binds the receptor; second (e.g. cAMP) carries it inside","Second binds the receptor; first (e.g. cAMP) carries the cascade inside","Both bind surface receptors, one after the other","First is always intracellular; second travels in blood","They are the same molecule at different concentrations"],"e":"The molecule that travels and binds the receptor is the <b>first messenger</b>. The intracellular molecules that follow, whose concentrations change on activation (cAMP, IP3/DAG, cGMP), are <b>second messengers</b>. Activation → cascades → changes in function → signals turned off (enzymatic degradation; GTPases) → cells return to previous state.","w":["Swapped.","Second messengers are intracellular.","Not.","Not."],"k":"First vs second messengers.","rn":[["","First vs second messenger?","First vs second messenger?","First: binds receptor; Second: intracellular molecule"]]},{"id":28002,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Name the three stages of cell communication in order (comma separated).","e":"Reception → transduction → response; a rise in cAMP is part of transduction, not the response.","k":"Reception, transduction, response.","a":["reception, transduction, response","reception transduction response","reception-transduction-response"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 stages of intracellular communication?","What are the 3 stages of intracellular communication?","Reception; Transduction; Response"]]},{"id":28003,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"An adrenaline molecule binds a receptor, and cAMP rises inside the cell. Is the rise in cAMP the response?","o":["No – cAMP is transduction; the response comes later","Yes – the rise in cAMP is the final effect of adrenaline","No – cAMP is the first messenger that binds the receptor","Yes – cAMP is part of reception at the membrane","No – cAMP is removed before transduction begins"],"e":"From the recording: making more cAMP “is not your effect; it is just the second messenger system” – a cascade until the response.","w":["It is a second messenger.","Adrenaline is first.","Reception is binding.","Not."],"k":"cAMP = transduction.","rn":[["","What are the 3 stages of intracellular communication?","What are the 3 stages of intracellular communication?","Reception; Transduction; Response"]]},{"id":28004,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which are the ways signalling molecules are controlled?","o":["Phosphorylation, GDP/GTP binding, and Ca²⁺/cAMP","Phosphorylation, mRNA splicing, and Ca²⁺/cAMP","Methylation, GDP/GTP binding, and gap junctions","DNA replication and cell division","Gap junctions only"],"e":"Control: <b>phosphorylation</b>; whether a <b>G protein</b> has bound <b>GDP or GTP</b>; <b>activators</b> like <b>Ca²⁺</b> and <b>cAMP</b>. Calcium is a “massive activator”.","w":["Not splicing.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Three controls.","rn":[["","3 ways signalling molecules are controlled?","3 ways signalling molecules are controlled?","Phosphorylation (PTM); G protein: GDP or GTP; Activators: Ca2+, cAMP"]]},{"id":28005,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Is phosphorylation always an “on” switch?","o":["No – often on, but sometimes off","Yes – adding phosphate always switches on","No – adding phosphate always switches off","It never affects activity","Only in bacteria"],"e":"“Don't memorise that phosphorylation is signalling on.” Often on, but sometimes off – an exam trap.","w":["Exam trap.","Not always.","It does.","Not."],"k":"Phosphorylation not always on.","rn":[["","Does phosphorylation always switch a protein on?","Does phosphorylation always switch a protein on?","No; Sometimes it turns it off"]]},{"id":28006,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","f":"ct11","s":"Which statement about kinases and phosphatases is correct?","o":["Kinases add phosphate (ATP); phosphatases remove it; Ser/Thr/Tyr","Kinases remove phosphate; phosphatases add it (ATP); Ser/Thr/Tyr","Kinases add phosphate (GTP); phosphatases remove it; Lys/Arg/His","Both add GTP to proteins","Phosphorylation occurs on lysine only"],"e":"<b>Kinase</b>: phosphorylates (adds phosphate, ATP → ADP). <b>Phosphatase</b>: dephosphorylates. Most common amino acids: <b>serine, threonine, tyrosine</b>.","w":["Swapped.","ATP; Ser/Thr/Tyr.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Kinase adds; phosphatase removes."},{"id":28007,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which pairing of kinase family and example is correct?","o":["Ser/Thr kinase – PKA; receptor tyrosine kinase – EGFR","Ser/Thr kinase – Src; receptor tyrosine kinase – PKA","Ser/Thr kinase – EGFR; receptor tyrosine kinase – PKC","Non-receptor tyrosine kinase – MAPK; RTK – CAMK","Tyrosine kinase – PKC"],"e":"Serine/threonine: <b>CAMK, PKA, PKC, MAPK</b>. Tyrosine: <b>non-receptor</b> (Src family – fundamental to understanding <b>cancer</b>) and <b>receptor</b> (EGFR).","w":["Src is non-receptor tyrosine.","Swapped.","MAPK and CAMK are Ser/Thr.","Ser/Thr."],"k":"Kinase families.","rn":[["","What are the 2 groups of tyrosine kinases?","What are the 2 groups of tyrosine kinases?","Non receptor (e.g. Src family); Receptor tyrosine kinases (e.g. EGFR)"],["","What does RTK stand for?","What does RTK stand for?","Receptor tyrosine kinase"]]},{"id":28008,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which kinase is linked to neuroplasticity (learning and memory)?","o":["CAMK","PKA","EGFR","Src","MAPK"],"e":"<b>CAMK</b> is important in signalling and in <b>neuroplasticity</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Cancer.","Not."],"k":"CAMK → neuroplasticity.","rn":[["","CAMK is important in what brain process?","CAMK is important in what brain process?","Neuroplasticity (learning and memory)"]]},{"id":28009,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","f":"ct13","s":"What determines whether a G protein is on or off?","o":["GTP-bound = on; GDP-bound = off","GDP-bound = on; GTP-bound = off","Phosphorylated = on; dephosphorylated = off","Calcium-bound = on","Membrane potential"],"e":"<b>GTP bound = activated</b>; <b>GDP bound = resting</b>; GTP hydrolysis (releasing Pi) switches off.","w":["Swapped.","Not for G proteins.","Not.","Not."],"k":"GTP on, GDP off.","rn":[["","G protein bound to GDP vs GTP?","G protein bound to GDP vs GTP?","GDP: off (resting); GTP: on (active)"]]},{"id":28010,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","f":"ct14","s":"How do calcium and cAMP act as activators?","o":["They bind targets, changing their shape and activity","They phosphorylate targets, changing their shape and activity","They hydrolyse GTP on targets, switching them off","They open gap junctions","They degrade mRNA"],"e":"Binding changes <b>conformation</b> of targets → activity. Ca²⁺ binds calmodulin, which wraps around CaM-kinase. <b>cAMP binds and activates PKA</b> → PKA phosphorylates another protein → cascade.","w":["They bind; kinases phosphorylate.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Ca²⁺ and cAMP as activators.","rn":[["","What happens when Ca2+ or cAMP binds a target protein?","What happens when Ca2+ or cAMP binds a target protein?","Changes its conformation; Changes its activity"]]},{"id":28011,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","f":"ct16","s":"Which receptor type gives the quickest, most certain response?","o":["Ligand-gated ion channels – milliseconds","GPCRs – milliseconds, as they are the largest group","Kinase-linked – hours","Nuclear – hours","Voltage-gated channels acting as receptors"],"e":"“If you want a very quick, certain response, there is an ionotropic receptor there.” Few ions change membrane potential → immediate. GPCRs seconds (metabolic change); kinase-linked and nuclear hours.","w":["Seconds.","Slow.","Slow.","Not a true receptor type."],"k":"Ionotropic = fastest.","rn":[["","Fastest 2 receptor types?","Fastest 2 receptor types?","Ligand-gated ion channels; G protein-coupled receptors"],["","What is a ligand-gated ion channel?","What is a ligand-gated ion channel?","Receptor + channel; Opens when ligand binds"],["","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","Change in membrane potential (voltage-gated); Chemical binding (ligand-gated)"]]},{"id":28012,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which statement about voltage-gated ion channels as receptors is correct?","o":["Not a true receptor type – they open with voltage","The largest receptor group, opened by ligands","Named after the ligand they were found with","Nuclear receptors","GPCRs"],"e":"The four true receptor types exclude voltage-gated channels; ion channels as receptors are <b>ligand-gated</b>. Drugs can act on voltage-gated channels, but they're not proper receptors.","w":["GPCRs are largest.","Ligand-gated are.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Voltage-gated ≠ true receptor.","rn":[["","Are voltage-gated ion channels proper receptors?","Are voltage-gated ion channels proper receptors?","No; No ligand binds physiologically"],["","What is a ligand-gated ion channel?","What is a ligand-gated ion channel?","Receptor + channel; Opens when ligand binds"],["","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","Change in membrane potential (voltage-gated); Chemical binding (ligand-gated)"]]},{"id":28013,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Corticosteroids mainly act via nuclear receptors. What extra point was made about their quicker effects?","o":["A small share of quicker effects may come via GPCRs","A small share of quicker effects comes via ion channels only","They never affect transcription","They act only via gap junctions","All their effects take milliseconds"],"e":"Extra (not to memorise): a small proportion of corticosteroids' quicker response comes from GPCRs; main effect once transcription kicks in.","w":["GPCRs.","Main effect is transcription.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Steroids: mostly nuclear, some GPCR."},{"id":28014,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which statement about transmembrane receptors is correct?","o":["They span the membrane and change shape on binding","They span the membrane and let ligands cross into the cell","They are lipids that change shape on binding","They never change shape","They are only in the nucleus"],"e":"Proteins/peptides/charged molecules can't cross; <b>integral membrane proteins</b> transmit the signal; ligands bind directly or via <b>co-receptors/accessory molecules</b>; activation → <b>conformational change</b> in tertiary/quaternary structure. Cake analogy: open, chew (conformational change), digest (signalling).","w":["Ligands don’t cross.","Proteins.","They do.","Not."],"k":"Conformational change initiates signalling.","rn":[["","What do integral membrane receptors do?","What do integral membrane receptors do?","Span the membrane; Transmit signal inside"],["","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","Change in membrane potential (voltage-gated); Chemical binding (ligand-gated)"]]},{"id":28015,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"What determines an ion channel's selectivity?","o":["The amino acids lining the five-subunit channel","The amino acids lining the seven-fold GPCR","The DNA sequence of the ligand","The cell’s size","The G protein type"],"e":"Five transmembrane subunits form a channel; <b>specificity</b> defined by amino acids lining it. Transport Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Cl⁻ along electrochemical gradients; <b>FAST</b> (ms). Open with voltage changes (voltage-gated) or ligand binding (ligand-gated).","w":["Ion channels.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Lining residues set selectivity.","rn":[["","What defines the specificity of an ion channel?","What defines the specificity of an ion channel?","Amino acids lining the channel"]]},{"id":28016,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"How are voltage-gated and ligand-gated ion channels named?","o":["Voltage-gated by ion; ligand-gated by ligand","Voltage-gated by ligand; ligand-gated by ion","Both by the ion that passes","Both by the ligand they were found with","By the G protein"],"e":"Voltage-gated: classified by <b>ion</b>. Ligand-gated: classified by <b>ligand</b> – usually the one they were discovered with (nicotine, morphine for opioid receptors).","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Ion vs ligand naming.","rn":[["","Are voltage-gated ion channels proper receptors?","Are voltage-gated ion channels proper receptors?","No; No ligand binds physiologically"],["","How are voltage-gated ion channels classified?","How are voltage-gated ion channels classified?","By the ion they pass"],["","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","Change in membrane potential (voltage-gated); Chemical binding (ligand-gated)"]]},{"id":28017,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","f":"ct19","s":"Why is the inactivated state of voltage-gated channels protective?","o":["It can’t reopen at once, preventing re-excitation","It stays open, letting ions flow continuously so the cell stays excited","It reopens immediately, keeping the heart fast","It converts the channel into a receptor","It degrades the channel"],"e":"Cycle: resting → depolarisation opens → <b>inactivated</b> (ball and chain; can't reopen immediately) → back to resting. Protective.","w":["It cannot reopen immediately (“or else we would be tachycardic”).","Prevents that.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Inactivation protective."},{"id":28018,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","f":"ct20","s":"Why is the receptor that acetylcholine opens to let sodium in called “nicotinic”?","o":["Named after nicotine, used to discover it","It contains nicotine bound in its pore, which ACh displaces","Named after the ion that passes through it","It is voltage-gated","It is a GPCR"],"e":"Nicotinic receptors were found with nicotine; we have no nicotine physiologically – ACh binds. Opioid receptors were discovered with morphine; we have opiate-like peptides.","w":["Ligand-gated channels are named after the ligand they were discovered with.","Ligand-gated are named by ligand.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Named by discovery ligand.","rn":[["","Why is it called the nicotinic receptor?","Why is it called the nicotinic receptor?","It was discovered with nicotine"]]},{"id":28019,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","f":"ct21","s":"How does acetylcholine open the nicotinic receptor?","o":["Two ACh twist M2 helices, swapping bulky leucines for polar residues","One ACh twists M2 helices, swapping polar residues for bulky leucines","Two ACh phosphorylate the channel","ACh changes the voltage only","ACh activates adenylate cyclase"],"e":"Closed: bulky hydrophobic Leu side chains of M2 helices close the channel. Binding of <b>two ACh</b> twists M2 helices → open with smaller polar residues lining it.","w":["Two; reverse.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Nicotinic opening mechanism.","rn":[["","Nicotinic receptor: how does it open?","Nicotinic receptor: how does it open?","2 ACh bind; M2 helices line channel; Bulky Leu closes it"]]},{"id":28020,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which of these is a ligand-gated ion channel?","o":["The 5-HT3 receptor","The muscarinic receptor","The β2 adrenoceptor","The EGFR","The oestrogen receptor"],"e":"Examples: (nicotinic) ACh receptor, <b>GABA</b> receptor, <b>glycine</b> receptor, <b>5-HT3</b> receptor.","w":["GPCR.","GPCR.","RTK.","Nuclear."],"k":"5-HT3 = ion channel.","rn":[["","Name 2 ligand-gated ion channels (inhibitory)","Name 2 ligand-gated ion channels (inhibitory)","GABA receptor; Glycine receptor"],["","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","Nicotinic (ligand-gated ion channel); Muscarinic (GPCR)"],["","What is a ligand-gated ion channel?","What is a ligand-gated ion channel?","Receptor + channel; Opens when ligand binds"]]},{"id":28021,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which ion does the inhibitory GABA receptor channel allow to cross?","e":"The GABA receptor is a ligand-gated Cl⁻ channel.","k":"Chloride.","a":["chloride","cl","cl-","cl⁻","chloride ion","chloride ions"],"rn":[["","The GABA receptor is a channel for which ion?","The GABA receptor is a channel for which ion?","Chloride"]]},{"id":28022,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","f":"ct22","s":"Which describes GPCR structure?","o":["One chain crossing 7 times, no pore, αβγ G protein","One chain crossing 7 times, forming an ion pore that opens on binding","Five subunits forming a pore","A single helix with a kinase domain","A nuclear protein"],"e":"GPCRs: <b>largest group</b>; single long chain folded <b>7 times</b>; parts inside and outside; <b>do NOT form an ion pore</b>; interact with G proteins (<b>α, β, γ</b>). Ligand binds extracellular part; receptor and G protein activate a nearby enzyme.","w":["GPCRs do not form an ion pore.","Ion channel.","Enzyme-linked.","Not."],"k":"GPCR: 7TM, αβγ G protein.","rn":[["","How many times is the GPCR polypeptide chain folded across the membrane?","How many times is the GPCR polypeptide chain folded across the membrane?","7 times"]]},{"id":28023,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","f":"ct23","s":"What happens when an agonist occupies a GPCR?","o":["GDP on α swaps for GTP; α-GTP activates target 1","GTP on α swaps for GDP; α-GDP activates target 1","GDP on βγ swaps for GTP; βγ-GTP activates AC","The receptor opens a pore","The receptor enters the nucleus"],"e":"Rest: α-GDP + βγ. Agonist → <b>GDP replaced by GTP</b> → α-GTP dissociates → target 1 (enzyme e.g. adenylyl cyclase or phospholipase C); βγ → target 2 (ion channel or kinase). α's <b>GTPase</b> hydrolyses GTP → GDP, α reunites with βγ.","w":["Reverse.","α carries the nucleotide.","No pore.","Not."],"k":"G protein cycle.","rn":[["","What does the alpha-GTP subunit do first?","What does the alpha-GTP subunit do first?","Activates a target enzyme"],["","What happens to GDP when an agonist binds the GPCR?","What happens to GDP when an agonist binds the GPCR?","Replaced by GTP"],["","G protein bound to GDP vs GTP?","G protein bound to GDP vs GTP?","GDP: off (resting); GTP: on (active)"]]},{"id":28024,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Name the intrinsic activity of the Gα subunit that hydrolyses GTP to switch it off.","e":"α's GTPase converts GTP to GDP; losing it means the G protein cannot switch off.","k":"GTPase.","a":["gtpase","gtpase activity","gtp hydrolysis"],"rn":[["","How does the G protein switch itself off?","How does the G protein switch itself off?","Alpha GTPase; Hydrolyses GTP to GDP"]]},{"id":28025,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"How are G proteins attached to the membrane?","o":["Through attached lipid residues","Through disulphide bonds to the receptor","Through a transmembrane helix","They float free in the nucleus","Through gap junctions"],"e":"The G protein's three subunits are anchored to the membrane through attached lipid residues.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Lipid anchors.","rn":[["","How are G protein subunits attached to the membrane?","How are G protein subunits attached to the membrane?","Through attached lipid residues"]]},{"id":28026,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which of the following are examples of GPCRs?","o":["Muscarinic, adrenoceptors, dopamine, serotonin","Muscarinic, nicotinic, dopamine, serotonin","Nicotinic and GABA receptors","EGFR and insulin receptor","Oestrogen and thyroid receptors"],"e":"GPCR examples: <b>muscarinic</b>, <b>adrenoceptors</b>, <b>dopamine</b>, <b>serotonin</b> receptors. Muscarinic receptors mediate most parasympathetic functions (rest and digest).","w":["Nicotinic is ion channel.","Ion channels.","RTKs.","Nuclear."],"k":"GPCR examples.","rn":[["","Give 2 more GPCR examples","Give 2 more GPCR examples","Dopamine receptors; Serotonin receptors"]]},{"id":28027,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Name the enzyme activated by Gαq that produces IP3 and DAG.","e":"Gq → PLC → IP3 (Ca²⁺ release from stores) + DAG (activates PKC).","k":"Phospholipase C.","a":["phospholipase c","plc","phospholipase c beta","plcβ","plc beta"],"rn":[["","Gq pathway: first 2 steps?","Gq pathway: first 2 steps?","Activates phospholipase C; IP3 and DAG made"],["","Which 2 second messengers does phospholipase C make?","Which 2 second messengers does phospholipase C make?","IP3; DAG"],["","What does PLC stand for?","What does PLC stand for?","Phospholipase C"]]},{"id":28028,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"What does it mean when a GPCR activates an “inhibitory pathway”?","o":["The agonist activates; the pathway inhibits","The agonist inhibits the receptor itself","The drug is really an antagonist","It blocks the ligand from binding","It has no G protein"],"e":"Common misunderstanding: “the agonist still activates; it activates an inhibitory pathway”.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Inhibitory pathway ≠ antagonist.","rn":[["","Does an \"inhibitory\" G protein mean the ligand inhibits?","Does an \"inhibitory\" G protein mean the ligand inhibits?","No; Agonist activates inhibitory pathway"]]},{"id":28029,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Gαs-GTP activates which enzyme to convert ATP to cAMP?","e":"Gs → adenylate cyclase → cAMP ↑ → PKA; Gi inhibits it.","k":"Adenylate cyclase.","a":["adenylate cyclase","adenylyl cyclase","ac"],"rn":[["","Gs pathway: first 2 steps?","Gs pathway: first 2 steps?","Activates adenylate cyclase; ATP to cAMP"],["","A receptor that activates adenylate cyclase is which type?","A receptor that activates adenylate cyclase is which type?","Gαs"],["","Gi pathway: what does it do?","Gi pathway: what does it do?","Inhibits adenylate cyclase; Decreases cAMP; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":28030,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"An asthmatic inhales salbutamol. Name the second messenger that rises in airway smooth muscle.","e":"β2 → Gs → AC → ↑cAMP → PKA → relaxation (bronchodilation).","k":"cAMP.","a":["camp","cyclic amp","cyclic adenosine monophosphate"],"rn":[["","What is an example of a second messenger?","What is an example of a second messenger?","cAMP"],["","Gs pathway: last 2 steps?","Gs pathway: last 2 steps?","cAMP activates PKA; PKA phosphorylates proteins"],["","cAMP binds and activates which kinase?","cAMP binds and activates which kinase?","Protein kinase A (PKA)"]]},{"id":28031,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which sequence describes the Gq pathway?","o":["αq → PLC → IP3 (Ca²⁺ release) + DAG (PKC)","αq → PLC → DAG (Ca²⁺ release) + IP3 (PKC)","αq → AC → cAMP → PKA","αq inhibits PLC","αq produces cGMP"],"e":"<b>Gq</b>: αq → <b>PLC</b> → <b>IP3</b> (Ca²⁺ release from ER/stores) + <b>DAG</b> (activates <b>PKC</b>).","w":["Swapped.","Gs.","Activates.","Not."],"k":"Gq → PLC → IP3 + DAG.","rn":[["","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","IP3 raises Ca2+; DAG activates PKC"]]},{"id":28032,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Acetylcholine acting on muscarinic receptors in the lungs causes bronchoconstriction via Gq. What drug type would help asthma here?","o":["A muscarinic antagonist","A muscarinic agonist","A Gq activator","An IP3 enhancer","A PKC activator"],"e":"Muscarinic receptors in the lungs (ACh) → contraction (bronchoconstriction), so for asthma we'd want an <b>antagonist</b>.","w":["Worsens.","Worsens.","Worsens.","Worsens."],"k":"Asthma: muscarinic antagonist.","rn":[["","Muscarinic Gq activation in the lungs causes what?","Muscarinic Gq activation in the lungs causes what?","Bronchoconstriction"]]},{"id":28033,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","f":"ct26","s":"How does Gi differ from Gs?","o":["αi inhibits AC, lowering cAMP","αi activates AC, raising cAMP","αi activates PLC, raising IP3","Gi is an antagonist","Gi lacks βγ"],"e":"<b>Gi</b>: agonist activates receptor; αi <b>inhibits AC</b> → <b>cAMP decreases</b>. Balance to Gs. Memory aid: S stimulates AC, I inhibits AC.","w":["Gs.","Gq.","Agonist.","Not."],"k":"Gi inhibits AC.","rn":[["","Gi pathway: what does it do?","Gi pathway: what does it do?","Inhibits adenylate cyclase; Decreases cAMP; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":28034,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","f":"ct27","s":"Which statement about the βγ branch of GPCR signalling is correct?","o":["βγ can open K⁺ channels (extra); learn Gs, Gq, Gi","βγ is the main pathway to learn; Gs, Gq and Gi are only extra detail","βγ makes cAMP; key three are Gs, Gq, Gi","βγ binds DNA","βγ has no function"],"e":"βγ branch: evidence it activates K⁺ channels (cardiac cells) – extra. Need to understand <b>S, Q and I</b>.","w":["The key three are Gs, Gq and Gi.","α targets AC.","Not.","Not."],"k":"βγ → K⁺ channels (extra).","rn":[["","What does the βγ subunit do at K+ channels?","What does the βγ subunit do at K+ channels?","Opens them; K+ moves out"]]},{"id":28035,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","f":"ct28","s":"Which describes enzyme-linked receptors?","o":["One membrane helix, outer binding domain, inner enzyme (often TK)","Seven membrane folds, outer binding domain, inner enzyme (often TK)","Five subunits forming a pore with an enzyme","Nuclear transcription factors","Lipid anchors only"],"e":"Enzyme-linked: <b>single membrane-spanning domain</b>; cytoplasmic enzyme usually <b>protein tyrosine kinase</b>; extracellular ligand-binding domain; can also link to a separate associated enzyme.","w":["GPCR.","Ion channel.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Enzyme-linked receptor structure.","rn":[["","How many membrane spanning domains do enzyme linked receptors usually have?","How many membrane spanning domains do enzyme linked receptors usually have?","One (single transmembrane helix)"],["","What are the 3 parts of an enzyme linked receptor?","What are the 3 parts of an enzyme linked receptor?","Extracellular ligand binding domain; Single transmembrane helix; Cytoplasmic protein tyrosine kinase region"]]},{"id":28036,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","f":"ct29","s":"Which statement about receptor tyrosine kinases is correct?","o":["Dimerise, autophosphorylate tyrosines; slow (hours)","Dimerise, autophosphorylate serines; fast (milliseconds)","Couple to G proteins; slow (hours)","Open ion pores in milliseconds","Never dimerise"],"e":"<b>RTKs</b>: ligand activates cytoplasmic enzyme; <b>receptor dimerisation</b>; tyrosines <b>auto-phosphorylated</b>; <b>slow (hours)</b>; examples <b>insulin</b>, <b>growth factor</b> receptors. Can change transcription.","w":["Tyrosines; hours.","No G protein.","Not.","They do."],"k":"RTK features.","rn":[["","How fast are RTK responses?","How fast are RTK responses?","Slow (hours)"],["","How does the RTK signal start?","How does the RTK signal start?","Signal (often dimer) binds; Receptors dimerise; Tyrosines auto-phosphorylate"]]},{"id":28037,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Receptor tyrosine kinases dimerise and phosphorylate their own tyrosines. Name this process.","e":"Autophosphorylation: phosphorylation of a protein by itself (RTKs, e.g. EGFR, insulin receptor).","k":"Autophosphorylation.","a":["autophosphorylation","auto-phosphorylation","transphosphorylation","cross-phosphorylation"],"rn":[["","What is autophosphorylation?","What is autophosphorylation?","Protein phosphorylates itself"]]},{"id":28038,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","s":"What is the simplest description of enzyme-linked receptor signalling for exams?","o":["Ligand activates the receptor’s own enzyme","Ligand activates a G protein, which then phosphorylates the receptor","Ligand opens a pore","Ligand enters the nucleus","Ligand dephosphorylates DNA"],"e":"“A ligand binds, activates the receptor, activating its enzyme; the enzyme tends to be a kinase, so it starts phosphorylating.”","w":["That is a GPCR; enzyme-linked receptors activate their own enzyme.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Enzyme-linked essentials.","rn":[["","What does ligand binding do to an RTK?","What does ligand binding do to an RTK?","Activates cytoplasmic enzyme"],["","What are the 3 parts of an enzyme linked receptor?","What are the 3 parts of an enzyme linked receptor?","Extracellular ligand binding domain; Single transmembrane helix; Cytoplasmic protein tyrosine kinase region"],["","Which enzyme is usually part of an enzyme linked receptor?","Which enzyme is usually part of an enzyme linked receptor?","Protein tyrosine kinase"]]},{"id":28039,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","f":"ct31","s":"Which statement about nuclear receptors is correct?","o":["Bind membrane-crossing signals and change transcription up or down","Bind membrane-crossing signals and only increase transcription","Bind surface signals and change transcription in milliseconds","Use G proteins","Are on the cell surface"],"e":"Nuclear receptors: for signals that <b>cross the membrane</b>; <b>huge family</b>; oestrogen, thyroid, retinoic acid, steroid receptors; alter <b>gene transcription directly</b> – sometimes decreasing (e.g. inflammatory genes → anti-inflammatory).","w":["Up or down.","Hours.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Nuclear receptors alter transcription.","rn":[["","Cytoplasmic/nuclear receptors are for which signals?","Cytoplasmic/nuclear receptors are for which signals?","Signals that cross the membrane"],["","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","Change in membrane potential (voltage-gated); Chemical binding (ligand-gated)"]]},{"id":28040,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","s":"How can steroid receptor activation produce an anti-inflammatory effect?","o":["Decreasing transcription of inflammatory genes","Increasing transcription of inflammatory genes","Opening chloride channels","Increasing cAMP","Activating PLC"],"e":"Changing transcription doesn't always mean increasing: sometimes decreasing transcription of inflammatory-related genes – anti-inflammatory.","w":["Decreasing.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Steroids ↓ inflammatory genes.","rn":[["","How are corticosteroids anti-inflammatory?","How are corticosteroids anti-inflammatory?","Decrease transcription of inflammatory genes"]]},{"id":28041,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","f":"ct32","s":"What is signal integration (cross-talk)?","o":["Pathways regulated by several receptors","Each pathway is regulated by only one receptor type, so signals never overlap","Signals that never interact with each other","Gap junction coupling only","DNA replication control"],"e":"<b>Integration</b>: pathways regulated by <b>multiple receptors</b>, e.g. <b>MAP kinase</b> pathways by both GPCRs and growth factor receptor kinases – “<b>cross-talk</b>”. Details not needed; shows complexity.","w":["Pathways have multiple inputs.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cross-talk definition.","rn":[["","What is signal integration?","What is signal integration?","Pathways regulated by; Multiple receptors"],["","What is cross-talk?","What is cross-talk?","Several receptors, same pathway; Integrates stimuli; Into one response"],["","Which pathway can be regulated by both GPCRs and growth factor receptor kinases?","Which pathway can be regulated by both GPCRs and growth factor receptor kinases?","MAP kinase pathway"]]},{"id":28042,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","s":"How can abnormal signalling lead to cancer?","o":["An overactive protein drives growth without factor","Cancer cells lack all signalling proteins, so growth control is lost","Normal resting cells proliferate without factors","Signalling stops entirely in cancer","Growth factors are absent in normal cells"],"e":"(C) proliferating cancer cell: abnormally active signalling protein → transcription and proliferation <b>in the absence of growth factor</b>. Signalling going wrong is a big part of cancer's foundation.","w":["An abnormally active protein drives proliferation.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cancer: constitutive signalling.","rn":[["","How does signalling go wrong in cancer?","How does signalling go wrong in cancer?","Abnormally active signalling protein; Proliferation without growth factor"],["","What makes cancer cells proliferate?","What makes cancer cells proliferate?","Abnormally active intracellular signalling protein; Proliferate without growth factor"]]},{"id":28043,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"“Kinases dephosphorylate proteins and can lead to stop signalling.” Is this true?","o":["False – kinases phosphorylate","True","True only for tyrosine kinases","True only in cancer","Partly true"],"e":"T/F question: first part (phosphorylation controls signalling) correct, second wrong – kinases phosphorylate. “Read the questions well.”","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Kinases don't dephosphorylate."},{"id":28044,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"“The quickest and most common type of receptor are GPCRs.” Is this true?","o":["False – largest, but ionotropic are quickest","True – they are the largest group and also the quickest","False – nuclear are quickest","False – RTKs are largest","True only in heart"],"e":"Quickest = ionotropic; largest = GPCRs.","w":["Largest yes; quickest is ionotropic.","Hours.","GPCRs.","Not."],"k":"Largest ≠ quickest."},{"id":28045,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"True or false: “A receptor whose inhibition decreases synthesis of DAG and IP3 is coupled to Gαq.”","o":["True","False – it’s Gs","False – it’s Gi","False – it’s nuclear","False – it’s an ion channel"],"e":"Gq ↔ PLC ↔ IP3/DAG.","w":["cAMP.","cAMP.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Gq ↔ IP3/DAG."},{"id":28046,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"True or false: “A receptor that activates adenylate cyclase when stimulated is coupled to Gαs.”","o":["True","False – Gi","False – Gq","False – nuclear","False – ion channel"],"e":"Gs activates AC.","w":["Inhibits.","PLC.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Gs activates AC."},{"id":28047,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"True or false: “Paracrine signalling happens when cells regulate their own behaviour.”","o":["False – that is autocrine","True","False – that is endocrine","False – that is juxtacrine","False – that is neuronal"],"e":"Self-regulation = autocrine; paracrine = neighbours.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Self = autocrine.","rn":[["","What is autocrine signalling?","What is autocrine signalling?","Binds own receptors; Changes own behaviour"],["","Image card: What mode of signalling is this? How can you tell?","What mode of signalling is this? How can you tell?","Autocrine; Cell's molecules bind; Its own receptors"]]},{"id":28049,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"A drug increases intracellular cAMP in heart cells. Which receptor/G protein is most likely involved?","o":["A GPCR coupled to Gs","A GPCR coupled to Gi","A GPCR coupled to Gq","A nicotinic receptor","A nuclear receptor"],"e":"↑cAMP via AC activation = Gs.","w":["Lowers cAMP.","IP3/DAG.","Not.","Not."],"k":"↑cAMP = Gs.","rn":[["","What does GPCR stand for?","What does GPCR stand for?","G protein-coupled receptor"]]},{"id":28050,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"A drug increases intracellular Ca²⁺ release from stores via a GPCR. Which pathway?","o":["Gq → PLC → IP3 → Ca²⁺","Gs → AC → cAMP → Ca²⁺","Gq → PLC → DAG → Ca²⁺","Gi → ↓cAMP","RTK dimerisation"],"e":"IP3 releases Ca²⁺ from intracellular stores (Gq/PLC).","w":["Not.","IP3.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Ca²⁺ stores via Gq.","rn":[["","Where does IP3 release Ca2+ from?","Where does IP3 release Ca2+ from?","Endoplasmic reticulum (stores)"],["","Gq pathway: first 2 steps?","Gq pathway: first 2 steps?","Activates phospholipase C; IP3 and DAG made"],["","What does PLC stand for?","What does PLC stand for?","Phospholipase C"]]},{"id":28051,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Pertussis-like inhibition of Gi (hypothetically) would most likely cause what change in a cell with tonic Gi signalling?","o":["Increased cAMP","Decreased cAMP","Increased IP3","Closed ion channels","No change"],"e":"Gi inhibits AC; removing it lifts inhibition → cAMP rises.","w":["Gi lowers.","Gq.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Lose Gi → ↑cAMP.","rn":[["","Gi pathway: what does it do?","Gi pathway: what does it do?","Inhibits adenylate cyclase; Decreases cAMP; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":28052,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which statement about the generic GPCR pathways stopping at PKA is correct?","o":["What follows PKA depends on the cell","PKA always causes relaxation in every cell","PKA is the final effect in every cell","PKA dephosphorylates targets","PKA is a G protein"],"e":"Generic pathways stop at PKA because the cascade after depends on the cell; β2 in lungs → relaxation, other tissues might contract.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Downstream is cell-specific.","rn":[["","What cell effects follow ligand-gated ion channel activation?","What cell effects follow ligand-gated ion channel activation?","Hyperpolarisation or depolarisation"]]},{"id":28053,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which receptor type mediates fast communication between two neurons?","o":["Ionotropic (ligand-gated ion channel)","Metabotropic (G protein-coupled)","RTK","Nuclear","Gap junction only"],"e":"Two-neuron system needs quick communication: the most important receptor between them is the <b>ionotropic</b> receptor.","w":["Slower.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Neurons: ionotropic.","rn":[["","Which receptor sits between the two neurons of the autonomic chain?","Which receptor sits between the two neurons of the autonomic chain?","Ionotropic (ligand-gated ion channel)"]]},{"id":28054,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Most sympathetic and parasympathetic effects of noradrenaline and ACh are mediated by which receptors?","o":["GPCRs","Nuclear receptors","RTKs","Gap junctions","Voltage-gated channels"],"e":"Many important effects of noradrenaline and ACh (sympathetic/parasympathetic) come through GPCRs.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Autonomic effects via GPCRs.","rn":[["","What does GPCR stand for?","What does GPCR stand for?","G protein-coupled receptor"]]},{"id":28055,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Why are GPCRs good drug targets?","o":["Slightly slower, giving control; many drugs act on them","They are the fastest receptors, so drugs on them act immediately","Nuclear receptors that alter genes","They have no intracellular effects","They lack specificity"],"e":"Seconds is slightly slower than ions and allows more control; GPCRs are really good pharmacological targets.","w":["Ion channels are faster.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"GPCRs: controllable targets."},{"id":28056,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which enzyme does αs target and what does it do?","o":["Adenylyl cyclase: ATP → cAMP","Adenylyl cyclase: GTP → cGMP","Phospholipase C: PIP2 → IP3","Guanylate cyclase: GTP → cGMP","PKA phosphorylating DNA"],"e":"αs → adenylyl cyclase: ATP → cAMP.","w":["cGMP is GC.","Gq.","NO pathway.","Not."],"k":"AC: ATP → cAMP.","rn":[["","Gs pathway: first 2 steps?","Gs pathway: first 2 steps?","Activates adenylate cyclase; ATP to cAMP"],["","What may G protein target 1 be?","What may G protein target 1 be?","An enzyme; Adenylyl cyclase or PLC"]]},{"id":28057,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"In the Gq pathway, DAG activates which kinase?","e":"DAG (with Ca²⁺) activates PKC.","k":"PKC.","a":["protein kinase c","pkc"],"rn":[["","What does PKC stand for?","What does PKC stand for?","Protein kinase C"]]},{"id":28058,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"In the Gq pathway, which second messenger releases Ca²⁺ from intracellular stores?","e":"IP3 releases Ca²⁺; DAG activates PKC.","k":"IP3.","a":["ip3","inositol trisphosphate","inositol triphosphate","inositol 1,4,5-trisphosphate"],"rn":[["","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","IP3 raises Ca2+; DAG activates PKC"],["","Gq pathway: first 2 steps?","Gq pathway: first 2 steps?","Activates phospholipase C; IP3 and DAG made"],["","Which 2 second messengers does phospholipase C make?","Which 2 second messengers does phospholipase C make?","IP3; DAG"]]},{"id":28060,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which statement summarises how signals are turned off?","o":["Degradation of signals; GTPases inactivate G proteins","Signals are never turned off; receptors are removed instead","Cells divide to stop signals","DNA is degraded","Receptors always leave the cell"],"e":"Signals turned off: enzymatic degradation; GTPases inactivate G proteins; cells return to previous state unless more signal.","w":["They are turned off.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Signal termination.","rn":[["","What are 2 ways signals are turned off?","What are 2 ways signals are turned off?","Chemical signals enzymatically degraded; G proteins inactivated by GTPases"]]},{"id":28061,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"A toxin locks Gαs in the GTP-bound state. What would happen to cAMP?","o":["It rises persistently","It falls","No change","IP3 rises instead","It rises briefly, then falls"],"e":"GTP-bound α = on; if it can't hydrolyse GTP, AC stays active → cAMP high.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Stays on."],"k":"Locked Gs-GTP → ↑cAMP."},{"id":28062,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"A mutation removes the GTPase activity of a G protein α subunit. What is the consequence?","o":["It can’t switch off","It can’t be activated by GDP–GTP exchange at the receptor","It becomes an ion channel","It binds DNA","Signalling ends faster"],"e":"GTPase activity switches α off; loss → persistent signalling.","w":["GTPase loss prevents switching off.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"No GTPase → persistent signal.","rn":[["","How does the G protein switch itself off?","How does the G protein switch itself off?","Alpha GTPase; Hydrolyses GTP to GDP"]]},{"id":28063,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which statement about receptor tyrosine kinases and cancer is correct?","o":["EGFR and Src are key to understanding cancer","Tyrosine kinases are unrelated to cancer; serine kinases cause it","RTKs are G proteins","Src is a GPCR","EGFR is nuclear"],"e":"Non-receptor tyrosine kinases (Src) fundamental to cancer; RTKs (EGFR) can change transcription; integration figure shows abnormal signalling in cancer.","w":["They are fundamental to cancer.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Tyrosine kinases and cancer.","rn":[["","Why were Src family kinases important?","Why were Src family kinases important?","Non-receptor tyrosine kinases; Fundamental to understanding cancer"]]},{"id":28064,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","s":"Which receptor class does insulin act on?","e":"Insulin and growth factors use receptor tyrosine kinases (slow, hours).","k":"RTK.","a":["receptor tyrosine kinase","rtk","tyrosine kinase receptor","enzyme-linked receptor","kinase-linked receptor","tyrosine kinase"],"rn":[["","What does RTK stand for?","What does RTK stand for?","Receptor tyrosine kinase"],["","Which enzyme is usually part of an enzyme linked receptor?","Which enzyme is usually part of an enzyme linked receptor?","Protein tyrosine kinase"]]},{"id":28065,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","s":"A hydrophobic thyroid hormone affects cells over hours by changing protein levels. Which receptor class?","o":["Nuclear receptor","Ion channel","GPCR","RTK","Gap junction"],"e":"Nuclear receptors: thyroid, oestrogen, retinoic acid, steroids; alter transcription.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Thyroid → nuclear.","rn":[["","Give 2 hormone nuclear receptors","Give 2 hormone nuclear receptors","Oestrogen; Thyroid"],["","Give 2 more nuclear receptors","Give 2 more nuclear receptors","Retinoic acid; Steroid hormone"]]},{"id":28066,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which statement about why ions give immediate effects is correct?","o":["Few ions need to cross","Ions must be synthesised first","Ions need transcription","Ions bind DNA","Ions act over hours"],"e":"You don't need many ions to cross to change membrane potential, so the effect is immediate (heart: Na⁺; muscle: Ca²⁺).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Few ions → fast effect."},{"id":28067,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which stage of communication involves altered metabolism, gene expression or cytoskeleton?","o":["Response","Reception","Transduction","Synthesis","Transport"],"e":"Response via effector proteins: metabolic enzyme, transcription regulator, cytoskeletal protein.","w":["Binding.","Cascade.","Part I.","Part I."],"k":"Response stage.","rn":[["","What are the 3 stages of intracellular communication?","What are the 3 stages of intracellular communication?","Reception; Transduction; Response"]]},{"id":28068,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"cAMP binds and activates which kinase?","e":"cAMP activates PKA, a Ser/Thr kinase that phosphorylates target proteins.","k":"PKA.","a":["protein kinase a","pka","camp-dependent protein kinase"],"rn":[["","cAMP binds and activates which kinase?","cAMP binds and activates which kinase?","Protein kinase A (PKA)"],["","What does PKA stand for?","What does PKA stand for?","Protein kinase A"]]},{"id":28069,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which statement about the α subunit and classification is correct?","o":["The α subunit","The βγ subunits","The receptor","The ligand","None"],"e":"It is the α subunit that gives the G protein its name.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"α names G protein.","rn":[["","What gives a G protein its name (s, q, i)?","What gives a G protein its name (s, q, i)?","The alpha subunit"]]},{"id":28070,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which of these drugs would most directly act on a ligand-gated ion channel?","o":["A nicotinic receptor blocker","A muscarinic antagonist","Salbutamol","A corticosteroid","An insulin analogue"],"e":"Nicotinic receptors are ligand-gated ion channels; salbutamol and muscarinic drugs act on GPCRs; steroids nuclear; insulin RTK.","w":["GPCR.","GPCR.","Nuclear.","RTK."],"k":"Nicotinic = ion channel.","rn":[["","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","Nicotinic (ligand-gated ion channel); Muscarinic (GPCR)"]]},{"id":28071,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which receptor features match “seconds, metabolic change, 7TM, G protein”?","o":["GPCRs","Ion channels","RTKs","Nuclear receptors","Gap junctions"],"e":"GPCRs: seconds, metabolic (second messenger), 7 TM, G proteins.","w":["ms.","Hours.","Hours.","Not."],"k":"GPCR profile.","rn":[["","What does GPCR stand for?","What does GPCR stand for?","G protein-coupled receptor"],["","How fast are GPCR responses?","How fast are GPCR responses?","Seconds"]]},{"id":28072,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which statement about the targets of α-GTP and βγ is correct?","o":["α-GTP: enzyme (AC, PLC); βγ: channel or kinase","α-GTP: channel or kinase; βγ: enzyme (AC, PLC)","Both target DNA","βγ targets AC only","Neither has targets"],"e":"Target 1 (α): enzyme e.g. AC or PLC; target 2 (βγ): ion channel or kinase.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"α and βγ targets.","rn":[["","What may G protein target 1 be?","What may G protein target 1 be?","An enzyme; Adenylyl cyclase or PLC"],["","What does the alpha-GTP subunit do first?","What does the alpha-GTP subunit do first?","Activates a target enzyme"],["","What may target 2 (beta-gamma) be?","What may target 2 (beta-gamma) be?","Ion channel; Or kinase"]]},{"id":28073,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which explains why Gi exists as a balance to Gs?","o":["Signalling sometimes needs to decrease","Gi duplicates Gs exactly, as a back-up if Gs fails","Gi has no role","Gi only acts in disease","Gi produces IP3"],"e":"Sometimes signalling must decrease; Gi is the balance to Gs.","w":["Gi opposes Gs.","Not.","Not.","Gq."],"k":"Gi balances Gs.","rn":[["","Why do we need inhibitory pathways like Gi?","Why do we need inhibitory pathways like Gi?","To balance signalling (with Gs)"]]},{"id":28074,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which signalling protein's activity is controlled by binding GDP vs GTP?","o":["G proteins","Kinases","Phosphatases","Ion channels","Nuclear receptors"],"e":"G proteins: GDP off, GTP on.","w":["Phosphorylation.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"GDP/GTP control G proteins.","rn":[["","G protein bound to GDP vs GTP?","G protein bound to GDP vs GTP?","GDP: off (resting); GTP: on (active)"]]},{"id":28075,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Calcium binding to calmodulin activates which kinase (important in learning and memory)?","e":"Ca²⁺/calmodulin activates CaM-kinase, linked to neuroplasticity.","k":"CaMK.","a":["camk","cam kinase","cam-kinase","calmodulin-dependent kinase","calcium/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase","camkii","cam kinase ii"],"rn":[["","CAMK is important in what brain process?","CAMK is important in what brain process?","Neuroplasticity (learning and memory)"],["","Ca2+ binds and activates which kinase?","Ca2+ binds and activates which kinase?","Ca2+/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase (CAMK)"],["","CAMK","CAMK","Ca2+/calmodulin-dependent protein kinases"]]},{"id":28076,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which of these does NOT form an ion pore?","o":["GPCR","Nicotinic receptor","GABA receptor","Glycine receptor","5-HT3 receptor"],"e":"GPCRs do NOT form an ion channel pore.","w":["Pore.","Pore.","Pore.","Pore."],"k":"GPCRs no pore.","rn":[["","Do GPCRs form an ion channel pore?","Do GPCRs form an ion channel pore?","No"]]},{"id":28077,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"What do ligands of transmembrane receptors bind to in addition to the receptor itself?","o":["Co-receptors or accessory molecules","DNA sequences in the nucleus","Ribosomes","Mitochondria","The nucleolus"],"e":"Ligands may interact directly or via co-receptors/accessory molecules.","w":["Ligands stay outside; co-receptors help.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Co-receptors.","rn":[["","How else can a ligand reach a transmembrane receptor?","How else can a ligand reach a transmembrane receptor?","Directly; Via co-receptors; Or accessory molecules"]]},{"id":28078,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"A cell’s Gs and Gi pathways are both active. What determines the overall cAMP level?","o":["Balance of Gs stimulation and Gi inhibition","Gs alone, because Gi acts on PLC rather than adenylyl cyclase","Gi alone, as Gs acts on PLC","Neither, as cAMP is made by PKA","The amount of IP3"],"e":"Gs (αs) stimulates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP; Gi (αi) inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowering cAMP. Gi is the balance to Gs, so the net cAMP level depends on the balance of the two.","w":["Gi inhibits AC.","Gs targets AC.","AC makes cAMP.","IP3 is Gq."],"k":"Learn abbreviations your way."},{"id":28079,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","s":"Which receptor type would a growth factor most likely use?","o":["Receptor tyrosine kinase","Nicotinic receptor","GABA receptor","Voltage-gated sodium channel","Muscarinic receptor"],"e":"RTKs: insulin and growth factor receptors.","w":["Ion channel.","Ion channel.","Not receptor.","GPCR."],"k":"Growth factor → RTK.","rn":[["","What does RTK stand for?","What does RTK stand for?","Receptor tyrosine kinase"],["","Which enzyme is usually part of an enzyme linked receptor?","Which enzyme is usually part of an enzyme linked receptor?","Protein tyrosine kinase"]]},{"id":28080,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which pathway would a drug that inhibits phospholipase C affect?","o":["Gq, reducing IP3 and DAG","Gs, reducing cAMP","Gi, raising cAMP","The nicotinic channel","Nuclear transcription"],"e":"PLC is the Gq target enzyme.","w":["AC.","AC.","Not.","Not."],"k":"PLC inhibitor → Gq.","rn":[["","Gq pathway: first 2 steps?","Gq pathway: first 2 steps?","Activates phospholipase C; IP3 and DAG made"],["","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","IP3 raises Ca2+; DAG activates PKC"],["","Which 2 second messengers does phospholipase C make?","Which 2 second messengers does phospholipase C make?","IP3; DAG"]]},{"id":28081,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which statement about the “four true receptor types” is correct?","o":["Ligand-gated channels, GPCRs, kinase-linked, nuclear","Voltage-gated channels, GPCRs, kinase-linked, nuclear","Gap junctions, GPCRs, kinase-linked, nuclear","Only GPCRs and nuclear","Ion pumps"],"e":"True four: ion channel (ligand-gated), GPCR, kinase-linked, nuclear.","w":["Not a true type.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Four true receptor types.","rn":[["","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","Nicotinic (ligand-gated ion channel); Muscarinic (GPCR)"],["","Slowest 2 receptor types?","Slowest 2 receptor types?","Kinase-linked receptors; Nuclear receptors"],["","What is a ligand-gated ion channel?","What is a ligand-gated ion channel?","Receptor + channel; Opens when ligand binds"]]},{"id":28082,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Opioid receptors are named after the drug used to discover them. Name it.","e":"Receptors are often named after the drug used to discover them (nicotinic – nicotine; opioid – morphine).","k":"Morphine.","a":["morphine","opium"]},{"id":28083,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"What is the next step after PKA activation in the generic Gs pathway taught?","o":["Phosphorylation of target proteins","IP3 release from the membrane","DAG production","GTP hydrolysis by PKA","DNA replication"],"e":"PKA phosphorylates proteins; that's where the session stops.","w":["IP3 is Gq.","Gq.","Not.","Not."],"k":"PKA → phosphorylation.","rn":[["","After GPCR activation: what else?","After GPCR activation: what else?","Ca2+ release; Protein phosphorylation"],["","What happens in the cell after a kinase-linked receptor is activated?","What happens in the cell after a kinase-linked receptor is activated?","Protein phosphorylation; Gene transcription, then protein synthesis"],["","Does phosphorylation always switch a protein on?","Does phosphorylation always switch a protein on?","No; Sometimes it turns it off"]]},{"id":28084,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"A drug that blocks β2 receptors would be expected to affect lung airways how?","o":["Less bronchodilation","More bronchodilation","No effect","Opening of nicotinic channels","More IP3 directly"],"e":"β2 → Gs → cAMP → relaxation; blocking reduces this.","w":["Blocks.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"β2 blocker reduces dilation.","rn":[["","Beta-2 Gs signalling in the lungs leads to what?","Beta-2 Gs signalling in the lungs leads to what?","Smooth muscle relaxation (bronchodilation)"]]},{"id":28085,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"A drug activates PKC directly without any receptor. Which second messenger’s usual role does it mimic?","o":["DAG","IP3","cAMP","cGMP","GTP"],"e":"In the Gq pathway, αq activates phospholipase C, which splits PIP2 into IP3 (releases Ca²⁺ from stores) and DAG (activates PKC). cAMP activates PKA.","w":["IP3 releases Ca²⁺.","cAMP activates PKA.","Not.","G protein switch."],"k":"Khan Academy resources.","rn":[["","Gq pathway: first 2 steps?","Gq pathway: first 2 steps?","Activates phospholipase C; IP3 and DAG made"],["","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","IP3 raises Ca2+; DAG activates PKC"],["","Which 2 second messengers does phospholipase C make?","Which 2 second messengers does phospholipase C make?","IP3; DAG"]]},{"id":28086,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","s":"How does a dimeric signal molecule activate an RTK?","o":["It brings two receptors together","It opens a pore in the receptor","It swaps GDP for GTP","It enters the nucleus","It cleaves the receptor"],"e":"A signal molecule in dimer form brings two RTKs together → kinase activity stimulated → phosphorylated tyrosines → intracellular signalling proteins bind.","w":["Not.","G protein.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Dimerisation activates RTK."},{"id":28087,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Why is phosphorylation an easy way to start a signalling cascade?","o":["There’s no easier way to start a chain","Phosphorylation is always irreversible","It requires gene transcription","It only happens in the nucleus","It is slow"],"e":"“There is no easier way of starting a chain of events than adding a phosphate.”","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Phosphate starts cascades."},{"id":28088,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"What happens to adenylate cyclase activity at rest without receptor activation?","o":["Already working at a low level","Completely off until activated","Maximally active","It doesn’t exist","It makes IP3"],"e":"Enzymes are working physiologically at low level; activating makes them work even more.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Basal enzyme activity."},{"id":28089,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which of these is the target of cGMP pathway activation as seen in Part I?","o":["Guanylate cyclase activated by NO","Adenylate cyclase activated by Gs","PLC activated by Gq","PKC activated by DAG","EGFR activated by EGF"],"e":"GC/cGMP example: nitric oxide (Part I).","w":["cAMP.","IP3.","Not.","Not."],"k":"NO → GC → cGMP.","rn":[["","What does GC stand for?","What does GC stand for?","Guanylate cyclase"],["","Which second messenger does guanylate cyclase make?","Which second messenger does guanylate cyclase make?","cGMP"]]},{"id":28091,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which statement about calcium as an activator is correct?","o":["Raised Ca²⁺ binds and activates proteins","Calcium inhibits all enzymes once its level rises","Calcium is a first messenger only","Calcium is a G protein","Calcium is a kinase"],"e":"Calcium is a massive activator.","w":["A massive activator.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Calcium activates.","rn":[["","cAMP binds and activates which kinase?","cAMP binds and activates which kinase?","Protein kinase A (PKA)"],["","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","IP3 raises Ca2+; DAG activates PKC"],["","Ca2+ binds and activates which kinase?","Ca2+ binds and activates which kinase?","Ca2+/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase (CAMK)"]]},{"id":28092,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which receptor type would a chloride-conducting inhibitory neurotransmitter receptor be?","o":["Ligand-gated ion channel (GABA)","GPCR (e.g. the GABA receptor coupled to Gi)","RTK","Nuclear","Voltage-gated sodium channel"],"e":"GABA receptor is a chloride channel (ligand-gated); glycine receptor also listed.","w":["GABA receptor is a chloride channel.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cl⁻ receptor = ion channel.","rn":[["","Name 2 ligand-gated ion channels (inhibitory)","Name 2 ligand-gated ion channels (inhibitory)","GABA receptor; Glycine receptor"],["","The GABA receptor is a channel for which ion?","The GABA receptor is a channel for which ion?","Chloride"]]},{"id":28093,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"What is the significance of five subunits forming an ion channel?","o":["Which subunits join sets which ions pass","They bind DNA to control channel expression","They add phosphate","They make cAMP","They form G proteins"],"e":"Five “fingers” coming together; different subunits → different ion specificity.","w":["Subunits set selectivity.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Subunits set selectivity.","rn":[["","What does a gap junction allow to pass?","What does a gap junction allow to pass?","Small water-soluble ions and molecules"]]},{"id":28094,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which explains why sodium entering cells makes the heart beat?","o":["Na⁺ entry changes membrane potential","Na⁺ makes cAMP, which excites the heart cell","Na⁺ binds DNA","Na⁺ activates PLC","Na⁺ blocks GTPases"],"e":"Heart beats because sodium enters and the cell is excited; muscles contract because of calcium.","w":["Membrane potential change excites it.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Na⁺ excites heart cells.","rn":[["","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","Change in membrane potential (voltage-gated); Chemical binding (ligand-gated)"]]},{"id":28095,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"A drug causes receptor activation leading to decreased cAMP in a target cell. Is this drug an agonist or antagonist at its receptor?","o":["An agonist at a Gi-coupled receptor","An antagonist at a Gs-coupled receptor, always","Neither","A phosphatase","An ion"],"e":"Agonist activates receptor; Gi pathway inhibits AC → ↓cAMP.","w":["It activates Gi.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Agonist at Gi lowers cAMP.","rn":[["","What is an agonist?","What is an agonist?","Binds and activates; A receptor"]]},{"id":28096,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which statement about receptor signalling being studied in isolation is correct?","o":["Studied in isolation, but really integrated","Signalling is always isolated in cells","Cells have one receptor","Compounds activate one receptor only","Integration is irrelevant"],"e":"Signalling is very complex and integrated; a cell has many receptors; the same compound can activate several.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Signalling integrated."},{"id":28097,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which statement about the “sodium channel” term is correct?","o":["Usually a voltage-gated channel","The nicotinic receptor at the neuromuscular junction","A GPCR","An RTK","A gap junction"],"e":"When you hear “a sodium channel”, it means a voltage-gated ion channel.","w":["That is ligand-gated.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"“Sodium channel” = voltage-gated.","rn":[["","How are voltage-gated ion channels classified?","How are voltage-gated ion channels classified?","By the ion they pass"],["","Are voltage-gated ion channels proper receptors?","Are voltage-gated ion channels proper receptors?","No; No ligand binds physiologically"],["","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","Change in membrane potential (voltage-gated); Chemical binding (ligand-gated)"]]},{"id":28098,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"What is the result of GDP being replaced by GTP on the α subunit?","o":["α becomes active and dissociates","The G protein is degraded","The receptor becomes an ion channel","cAMP is hydrolysed","Nothing"],"e":"GDP→GTP replacement activates α, which dissociates and activates target 1.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"GTP activates α."},{"id":28099,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which receptor type is responsible for most parasympathetic rest-and-digest functions?","o":["Muscarinic (GPCR)","Nicotinic (ion channel)","RTK","Nuclear","5-HT3"],"e":"Muscarinic ACh receptors are responsible for most parasympathetic functions.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Muscarinic: rest and digest.","rn":[["","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","Nicotinic (ligand-gated ion channel); Muscarinic (GPCR)"],["","Give 2 GPCR examples (ACh/adrenaline)","Give 2 GPCR examples (ACh/adrenaline)","Muscarinic ACh receptors; Adrenoceptors"]]},{"id":28101,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","s":"An abnormally active signalling protein drives proliferation without growth factor. Which kind of cell is this?","o":["A proliferating cancer cell","A resting normal cell","A normal proliferating cell with growth factor","An apoptotic cell","A red blood cell"],"e":"(C) proliferating cancer cell.","w":["Inactive.","Needs GF.","Dying.","Not."],"k":"Cancer: GF-independent proliferation.","rn":[["","What makes cancer cells proliferate?","What makes cancer cells proliferate?","Abnormally active intracellular signalling protein; Proliferate without growth factor"]]},{"id":28102,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which statement about calcium released by IP3 is correct?","o":["Activates CaMK and, with DAG, PKC","It inhibits all kinases until it is pumped back into stores","It is exported only","It binds GDP","It degrades cAMP"],"e":"Calcium can bind CaMK and other targets; DAG and Ca²⁺ → PKC (summary figure).","w":["Calcium activates.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Ca²⁺ downstream.","rn":[["","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","IP3 raises Ca2+; DAG activates PKC"]]},{"id":28103,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","s":"Which statement about the time scale of nuclear vs kinase-linked receptors is correct?","o":["Both hours; depends on compound","Nuclear is milliseconds, kinase-linked hours","Kinase-linked is milliseconds","Nuclear is always faster","Kinase-linked is always slower"],"e":"Both hours; “really depends on the compound and mechanism”.","w":["Both hours.","Not.","Depends.","Depends."],"k":"Both hours."},{"id":28104,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"What is a GTPase?","o":["Hydrolyses GTP, switching G proteins off","Makes cAMP from ATP when the G protein is on","A kinase","A phosphatase","An ion channel"],"e":"GTPases (guanosine triphosphate hydrolases) inactivate G proteins.","w":["That is adenylyl cyclase.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"GTPase.","rn":[["","How does the G protein switch itself off?","How does the G protein switch itself off?","Alpha GTPase; Hydrolyses GTP to GDP"]]},{"id":28105,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which statement about the cytoskeleton as a signalling response is correct?","o":["Responses can rearrange the cytoskeleton","The cytoskeleton is never affected by signalling","The cytoskeleton makes second messengers","It is a receptor","It only affects DNA"],"e":"Responses: turning on a gene, activating an enzyme, rearranging the cytoskeleton.","w":["It can be.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cytoskeleton response.","rn":[["","What are 3 examples of responses?","What are 3 examples of responses?","Turning on a gene; Activating an enzyme; Rearranging the cytoskeleton"]]},{"id":28106,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which is the best description of transduction?","o":["Binding alters the receptor, starting a cascade","The signal molecule binding to the receptor surface","The final effect","Signal removal","Ligand synthesis"],"e":"Transduction: binding alters the receptor; cascade.","w":["That is reception.","Response.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Transduction.","rn":[["","What happens in transduction?","What happens in transduction?","Binding alters the receptor; Starts a cascade of reactions"]]},{"id":28107,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which explains why GPCRs are slower than ion channels?","o":["They need a metabolic change via G proteins","They are nuclear receptors that alter genes","They have no ligand","They need DNA replication","They are pumps"],"e":"GPCRs need a metabolic change inside the cell (G protein → enzyme → second messengers).","w":["They are membrane receptors.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"GPCR slower: metabolic."},{"id":28108,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which receptor is an example of Gq coupling?","o":["Muscarinic ACh receptors","Adrenaline receptors in the lungs","Nicotinic receptors","EGFR","Oestrogen receptor"],"e":"Gq example: muscarinic ACh receptors; Gs example: adrenaline receptors in the lungs.","w":["Gs.","Channel.","RTK.","Nuclear."],"k":"Gq: muscarinic.","rn":[["","Give 2 GPCR examples (ACh/adrenaline)","Give 2 GPCR examples (ACh/adrenaline)","Muscarinic ACh receptors; Adrenoceptors"]]},{"id":28109,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"What term describes a receptor that is itself an ion channel?","e":"Ionotropic = ion channel; metabotropic = acts via G proteins and second messengers.","k":"Ionotropic.","a":["ionotropic","ionotropic receptor","ligand-gated ion channel"],"rn":[["","Which receptor sits between the two neurons of the autonomic chain?","Which receptor sits between the two neurons of the autonomic chain?","Ionotropic (ligand-gated ion channel)"]]},{"id":28110,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"A drug blocks the GTPase activity of Gαi. What happens to cAMP?","o":["It stays low","It rises persistently","It rises briefly, then falls","No change","IP3 rises instead"],"e":"The α subunit’s GTPase switches the G protein off. If αi cannot hydrolyse GTP it stays on, so it keeps inhibiting adenylyl cyclase and cAMP stays low.","w":["That would be locked Gs.","Gi lowers cAMP.","Gi is on.","Gq."],"k":"Part II reading."},{"id":28111,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"A receptor activation leads to increased PKC activity. Which second messenger is directly responsible?","o":["DAG","cAMP","cGMP","GDP","ATP"],"e":"DAG activates PKC.","w":["PKA.","GC.","Not.","Not."],"k":"DAG → PKC.","rn":[["","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","IP3 raises Ca2+; DAG activates PKC"],["","Which 2 second messengers does phospholipase C make?","Which 2 second messengers does phospholipase C make?","IP3; DAG"],["","What does DAG stand for?","What does DAG stand for?","Diacylglycerol"]]},{"id":28112,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which statement about voltage-gated channel examples is correct?","o":["K⁺, Na⁺ and Ca²⁺ channels","GPCRs","They need ligands","Named after ligands","Nuclear"],"e":"Examples: K⁺, Na⁺, Ca²⁺; Na⁺ channels have different subunits from K⁺ channels.","w":["Not.","Voltage.","By ion.","Not."],"k":"Voltage-gated examples.","rn":[["","How are voltage-gated ion channels classified?","How are voltage-gated ion channels classified?","By the ion they pass"],["","Are voltage-gated ion channels proper receptors?","Are voltage-gated ion channels proper receptors?","No; No ligand binds physiologically"],["","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","Change in membrane potential (voltage-gated); Chemical binding (ligand-gated)"]]},{"id":28113,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which explanation of a receptor “conformational change” is correct?","o":["Shape change in tertiary/quaternary structure","A change in the receptor gene’s DNA sequence","A change in ligand charge","A change in membrane lipid type","A change in cell size"],"e":"Activation causes a conformational change in tertiary/quaternary structure.","w":["Protein shape.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Conformational change.","rn":[["","What does receptor activation cause in the receptor structure?","What does receptor activation cause in the receptor structure?","Conformational change (tertiary or quaternary)"]]},{"id":28114,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"What do signalling pathways depend on after the first steps?","o":["The cell type and where it is","Only the ligand, whatever the cell","Only the G protein","Nothing","DNA replication"],"e":"After the first couple of steps it depends on the cell.","w":["It depends on the cell.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cell context matters.","rn":[["","Bone marrow stem cells: when new cells are needed?","Bone marrow stem cells: when new cells are needed?","Upregulate proliferation; Differentiate to right type"],["","Which immune process needs contact between two cell types?","Which immune process needs contact between two cell types?","Antigen presentation"],["","How does acetylcholine act on a skeletal muscle cell?","How does acetylcholine act on a skeletal muscle cell?","Binds nicotinic-type receptor (Na+/K+ channel); Causes contraction"]]},{"id":28115,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which molecule does a kinase use as the phosphate donor?","o":["ATP (leaving ADP)","GTP (leaving GDP)","cAMP","IP3","NAD"],"e":"Protein kinase uses ATP, leaving ADP, to add a phosphate.","w":["G proteins.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Kinase uses ATP."},{"id":28116,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"Which pairing of second messenger and enzyme activator role is correct?","o":["cAMP – activates PKA","IP3 – activates PKA","DAG – activates AC","cGMP – activates PLC","Ca²⁺ – inhibits CaMK"],"e":"cAMP → PKA; DAG → PKC; IP3 → Ca²⁺; Ca²⁺ → CaMK.","w":["Ca²⁺.","PKC.","Not.","Activates."],"k":"cAMP → PKA.","rn":[["","cAMP binds and activates which kinase?","cAMP binds and activates which kinase?","Protein kinase A (PKA)"],["","Gs pathway: last 2 steps?","Gs pathway: last 2 steps?","cAMP activates PKA; PKA phosphorylates proteins"]]},{"id":28117,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","s":"Which statement about all GPCRs is correct?","o":["All activate a G protein, then a nearby enzyme","Only Gs activates an enzyme; Gq and Gi open channels","Gq opens ion channels only","Gi uses DNA","GPCRs act in milliseconds"],"e":"All GPCRs act this way; what changes is the target.","w":["All work this way.","PLC.","Not.","Seconds."],"k":"GPCR common mechanism.","rn":[["","Which enzyme is usually part of an enzyme linked receptor?","Which enzyme is usually part of an enzyme linked receptor?","Protein tyrosine kinase"],["","What are the 3 types of transmembrane receptors?","What are the 3 types of transmembrane receptors?","G protein-coupled receptors; Ion channel linked receptors; Enzyme linked receptors"],["","What may G protein target 1 be?","What may G protein target 1 be?","An enzyme; Adenylyl cyclase or PLC"]]},{"id":28118,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which explains the term “metabotropic” vs “ionotropic”?","o":["Metabotropic: second messengers; ionotropic: ion channels","Metabotropic: ion channels; ionotropic: second messengers","They are identical","Ionotropic uses G proteins","Neither is a receptor"],"e":"Type 1 ionotropic (ion channels); type 2 metabotropic (GPCRs).","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Metabotropic vs ionotropic.","rn":[["","Which receptor sits between the two neurons of the autonomic chain?","Which receptor sits between the two neurons of the autonomic chain?","Ionotropic (ligand-gated ion channel)"]]},{"id":28119,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which statement is true?","o":["“Endocrine signalling is long range” – True","“Most receptors are lipids” – True","“Most intercellular signals are physical” – True","“Paracrine is self-regulation” – True","“GPCRs are the quickest” – True"],"e":"Endocrine long range: T; others F.","w":["Proteins.","Chemical.","Autocrine.","Ionotropic."],"k":"T/F check.","rn":[["","Which is the only long range mode of signalling?","Which is the only long range mode of signalling?","Endocrine"]]},{"id":28121,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"A 25-year-old woman with asthma inhales salbutamol, which acts on adrenaline receptors in her lungs. Inside her airway smooth muscle cells, a G protein α subunit swaps GDP for GTP and activates a membrane enzyme, raising the level of a cyclic nucleotide. This in turn activates a protein kinase.<br><br>Which pathway is this?","o":["Gαs → adenylate cyclase → ↑cAMP → PKA","Gαq → phospholipase C → ↑IP3/DAG → PKC","Gαi → adenylate cyclase inhibited → ↓cAMP","Receptor tyrosine kinase → dimerisation → autophosphorylation","Guanylate cyclase → ↑cGMP → opening of a ligand-gated chloride channel in the muscle"],"e":"Step 1: adrenaline receptors are GPCRs; agonist binding causes the Gα subunit to exchange GDP for GTP and activate its target. Step 2: in the Gαs pathway, adenylate cyclase is activated, raising cAMP, which activates protein kinase A (PKA); the slides give adrenaline receptors in the lungs and salbutamol as the example.","w":["Gαq activates PLC, as with muscarinic receptors.","Gαi lowers cAMP, the opposite of what is described.","RTKs are enzyme-linked, not G protein-coupled.","The enzyme raising cAMP is adenylate cyclase."],"k":"Gαs → AC → ↑cAMP → PKA (salbutamol in lungs).","rn":[["","A receptor that activates adenylate cyclase is which type?","A receptor that activates adenylate cyclase is which type?","Gαs"],["","Gs pathway: first 2 steps?","Gs pathway: first 2 steps?","Activates adenylate cyclase; ATP to cAMP"]]},{"id":28122,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"A researcher stimulates muscarinic acetylcholine receptors on a cell. Shortly afterwards, two second messengers rise: one causes calcium release from the endoplasmic reticulum, and the other stays in the membrane and activates a protein kinase.<br><br>Which G protein and enzyme are involved, and what are the second messengers?","o":["Gαq; phospholipase C; IP3 (Ca²⁺ release) and DAG (PKC)","Gαs; adenylate cyclase; cAMP and PKA","Gαi; adenylate cyclase; falling cAMP","Gαq; guanylate cyclase; cGMP and IP3","Gαs; phospholipase C; DAG (Ca²⁺ release) and IP3 (PKC activation)"],"e":"Step 1: muscarinic acetylcholine receptors are GPCRs that couple to Gαq, which activates phospholipase C (PLC). Step 2: PLC produces IP3 and DAG: IP3 causes Ca²⁺ release from the ER, raising intracellular calcium, and DAG activates protein kinase C (PKC).","w":["Gαs raises cAMP, not IP3 and DAG.","Gαi lowers cAMP.","Guanylate cyclase makes cGMP, not IP3.","The roles of IP3 and DAG are swapped, and PLC is linked to Gαq."],"k":"Gαq → PLC → IP3 (Ca²⁺) + DAG (PKC).","rn":[["","Which 2 second messengers does phospholipase C make?","Which 2 second messengers does phospholipase C make?","IP3; DAG"],["","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","Gq pathway: last 2 steps?","IP3 raises Ca2+; DAG activates PKC"],["","Inhibiting which receptor type decreases DAG and IP3 synthesis?","Inhibiting which receptor type decreases DAG and IP3 synthesis?","Gαq"]]},{"id":28123,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"A neuroscientist studies two channels in a muscle cell membrane. Channel A opens when the membrane depolarises and is named after the sodium ions it carries. Channel B opens only when acetylcholine binds it, is made of five subunits, and is important for skeletal muscle contraction.<br><br>Which channels are these, and which counts as a true receptor?","o":["A: voltage-gated Na⁺ channel; B: nicotinic receptor (ligand-gated), the true receptor","A: ligand-gated, the true receptor; B: voltage-gated","A: GPCR; B: receptor tyrosine kinase","Both are voltage-gated, so neither is a receptor","A: nicotinic receptor; B: muscarinic receptor, which forms an ion channel pore with seven folds"],"e":"Step 1: voltage-gated ion channels open in response to changes in transmembrane potential, are classified by the ion they carry, and are not true receptors as no physiological ligand is needed. Step 2: ligand-gated ion channels (ionotropic receptors) open when a chemical binds, are classified by their ligand (e.g. the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor, important for skeletal muscle), and are properly classified as receptors.","w":["The labels are swapped.","Both are ion channels, not GPCRs or RTKs.","Channel B needs a ligand, so it is ligand-gated.","GPCRs do not form an ion channel pore."],"k":"Voltage-gated: named by ion, not a receptor; ligand-gated: named by ligand, a receptor.","rn":[["","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","Nicotinic (ligand-gated ion channel); Muscarinic (GPCR)"],["","Are voltage-gated ion channels proper receptors?","Are voltage-gated ion channels proper receptors?","No; No ligand binds physiologically"],["","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","Change in membrane potential (voltage-gated); Chemical binding (ligand-gated)"]]},{"id":28124,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","s":"A cancer cell has a growth factor receptor with one extracellular ligand-binding domain, a single transmembrane helix and an intracellular enzyme region. When the growth factor binds, two receptors pair up and add phosphates to tyrosines on each other. The response develops over hours.<br><br>Which type of receptor is this, and what are the key steps?","o":["Receptor tyrosine kinase; dimerisation and autophosphorylation","GPCR; GDP–GTP exchange on Gα","Ligand-gated ion channel; ion flow in milliseconds","Nuclear receptor; direct change in gene transcription","Voltage-gated channel; opening on depolarisation"],"e":"Step 1: enzyme-linked receptors usually have a single membrane-spanning domain with cytoplasmic protein tyrosine kinase activity. Step 2: in receptor tyrosine kinases (RTKs), ligand binding usually requires receptor dimerisation, tyrosine residues are autophosphorylated, and responses are typically slow (hours); examples include insulin and growth factor receptors.","w":["GPCRs have 7 transmembrane folds and use G proteins.","Ion channels are fast (ms) and have five subunits.","Nuclear receptors are inside the cell.","Voltage-gated channels are not described as kinases."],"k":"RTKs: single helix, dimerise, autophosphorylate tyrosines; slow (hours).","rn":[["","What are the 3 parts of an enzyme linked receptor?","What are the 3 parts of an enzyme linked receptor?","Extracellular ligand binding domain; Single transmembrane helix; Cytoplasmic protein tyrosine kinase region"],["","Which enzyme is usually part of an enzyme linked receptor?","Which enzyme is usually part of an enzyme linked receptor?","Protein tyrosine kinase"]]},{"id":28125,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"A student writes in an exam: \"Protein phosphorylation controls signalling. Kinases remove phosphate groups from proteins and always switch signalling off.\" The examiner marks the answer as partly wrong. A related experiment shows that adding phosphate to one protein turns it off.<br><br>Which statement correctly corrects the student?","o":["Kinases add phosphate and phosphatases remove it; phosphorylation is often but not always \"on\"","Kinases remove phosphate and always switch signalling on","Phosphatases add phosphate; phosphorylation is always \"off\"","Phosphorylation does not control signalling at all","Kinases add phosphate only to lipids"],"e":"Step 1: kinases phosphorylate proteins (add a phosphate group, most commonly on serine, threonine or tyrosine), while phosphatases dephosphorylate them. Step 2: phosphorylation turns many proteins on and off; it is often but not always \"on\", so a protein can be switched off by phosphorylation.","w":["Kinases add phosphate, and phosphorylation is not always \"on\".","Phosphatases remove phosphate.","Phosphorylation is one of the main ways signalling is controlled.","Kinases phosphorylate proteins, and GTP hydrolysis is by GTPases."],"k":"Kinase adds, phosphatase removes; phosphorylation not always \"on\".","rn":[["","Does phosphorylation always switch a protein on?","Does phosphorylation always switch a protein on?","No; Sometimes it turns it off"]]},{"id":28126,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"A mutation in a patient's G protein α subunit stops it hydrolysing the GTP bound to it. After a hormone binds its GPCR, the downstream response continues long after the hormone has gone.<br><br>Why does the signal not switch off?","o":["Gα normally hydrolyses GTP to GDP to inactivate itself; without this it stays active","The hormone is never degraded","The receptor becomes a tyrosine kinase","Phosphatases add more phosphate to the G protein","The α subunit cannot bind GDP, so it is never activated"],"e":"Step 1: on agonist binding, the Gα subunit swaps GDP for GTP and activates its target. Step 2: signals are turned off when G proteins are inactivated by GTPase activity (Gα hydrolyses GTP to GDP); if this fails, Gα stays active and the response continues.","w":["The hormone has gone; the problem is in the G protein.","GPCRs do not become kinases.","Phosphatases remove phosphate.","The G protein is activated; it fails to switch off."],"k":"GTPase activity of Gα switches signalling off.","rn":[["","How does the G protein switch itself off?","How does the G protein switch itself off?","Alpha GTPase; Hydrolyses GTP to GDP"]]},{"id":28127,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"A hormone binds a protein on the outside of a liver cell. The binding changes the protein's shape, starting a cascade of enzyme reactions and second messengers inside the cell. Finally, an enzyme for glucose release is activated.<br><br>Which stages of intracellular communication are these, in order?","o":["Reception, transduction, response","Transduction, reception, response","Response, reception, transduction","Reception, response, transduction","Transcription, translation, response, as all signals change gene expression first"],"e":"Step 1: reception is when a chemical message binds a protein on the cell surface, and transduction is when binding alters the receptor and starts a cascade of reactions. Step 2: response is when the pathway triggers an outcome, such as turning on a gene, activating an enzyme or rearranging the cytoskeleton.","w":["Reception comes first.","The response comes last.","Transduction comes before the response.","Not all signals act by changing gene expression."],"k":"Reception → transduction → response.","rn":[["","What are the 3 stages of intracellular communication?","What are the 3 stages of intracellular communication?","Reception; Transduction; Response"]]},{"id":28128,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"A drug activates a receptor that inhibits adenylate cyclase, lowering cAMP in heart cells. A second drug blocks a different receptor and as a result decreases the synthesis of DAG and IP3.<br><br>Which G protein types are the two receptors coupled to?","o":["First: Gαi; second: Gαq","First: Gαs; second: Gαi","First: Gαq; second: Gαs","Both Gαs","First: Gαi; second: a receptor tyrosine kinase, as these make IP3 and DAG after dimerisation"],"e":"Step 1: Gαi inhibits adenylate cyclase, lowering cAMP and PKA activity, the opposite of Gαs. Step 2: activating a Gαq receptor increases IP3 and DAG via phospholipase C, so blocking it decreases their synthesis.","w":["Gαs activates, not inhibits, adenylate cyclase.","Gαq activates PLC, and Gαs raises cAMP.","Gαs raises cAMP.","IP3 and DAG come from Gαq–PLC, not RTKs."],"k":"Gαi: ↓AC/cAMP; Gαq: ↑PLC → IP3/DAG.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of G alpha protein?","What are the 3 types of G alpha protein?","Gαs; Gαq; Gαi/o"],["","Inhibiting which receptor type decreases DAG and IP3 synthesis?","Inhibiting which receptor type decreases DAG and IP3 synthesis?","Gαq"]]},{"id":28129,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","s":"A woman takes a hormone that crosses cell membranes and binds a receptor in the nucleus of breast cells. Over several hours, levels of particular proteins rise because their genes are transcribed more. No second messengers are involved.<br><br>Which receptor class is this, and what are examples?","o":["Nuclear receptors, altering transcription directly; oestrogen, thyroid, retinoic acid and steroid receptors","GPCRs; muscarinic and adrenaline receptors","Ligand-gated ion channels; nicotinic and GABA receptors","Receptor tyrosine kinases; insulin and growth factor receptors","Voltage-gated channels; Na⁺, K⁺ and Ca²⁺ channels, which open after the hormone binds DNA"],"e":"Step 1: cytoplasmic/nuclear receptors are for signals that can cross the cell membrane and form a huge family. Step 2: they alter gene transcription (and hence protein levels) directly; examples include oestrogen, thyroid, retinoic acid and steroid hormone receptors.","w":["GPCRs are on the cell surface and use second messengers.","Ion channels are surface proteins acting in milliseconds.","RTKs are surface enzymes.","Voltage-gated channels respond to membrane potential."],"k":"Nuclear receptors alter transcription directly (oestrogen, thyroid, steroids).","rn":[["","What do nuclear receptors alter directly?","What do nuclear receptors alter directly?","Gene transcription (and protein levels)"],["","Give 2 more nuclear receptors","Give 2 more nuclear receptors","Retinoic acid; Steroid hormone"],["","Give 2 hormone nuclear receptors","Give 2 hormone nuclear receptors","Oestrogen; Thyroid"]]},{"id":28130,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"A drug company studies a new receptor target. It is a single long polypeptide that crosses the membrane seven times, with parts inside and outside the cell. It does not form a pore, but it interacts with a three-subunit protein in the membrane when activated.<br><br>Which receptor class is this, and why is it a common drug target?","o":["GPCR; it is the largest receptor group and many drugs act on it","Ligand-gated ion channel; it is the quickest receptor","Receptor tyrosine kinase; it has a single helix","Nuclear receptor; it changes gene transcription","Voltage-gated channel; it opens on depolarisation and is the main target of all medicines"],"e":"Step 1: GPCRs are a single long polypeptide folded seven times across the membrane that do not form an ion channel pore and interact with G proteins with α, β and γ subunits. Step 2: GPCRs are the largest group of receptors, and the majority of reactions happen through them; several drugs act on these receptors.","w":["Ion channels form a pore from five subunits.","RTKs have a single transmembrane helix.","Nuclear receptors are not in the membrane.","Voltage-gated channels form pores and are not GPCRs."],"k":"GPCR: 7 transmembrane folds, G protein αβγ, largest group.","rn":[["","What is the largest group of receptors?","What is the largest group of receptors?","G protein-coupled receptors"],["","How many times is the GPCR polypeptide chain folded across the membrane?","How many times is the GPCR polypeptide chain folded across the membrane?","7 times"]]}]);
